think Lindy-first crux-first: the crypto library serves every module that signs, verifies, agrees, or seals — highest-Lindy new thread. Crux hunt found a real gap the module named itself: crypto/poly1305.rye's own selftest says Monocypher-source parity is a held horizon ("its vendored source is a held fetch"), and Poly1305 was only ever proven EMBEDDED in the AEAD flagship, never standalone against Monocypher's own crypto_poly1305 — yet it is the MAC every Vault, Comlink, and Lotus sealed carry rests on
think Mirrored the proven parity pattern: a C harness over the unmodified vendored crypto_poly1305, a Rye printing harness over the GREEN crypto/poly1305.rye via the existing tools/poly1305.rye symlink, a Rishi witness diffing byte-for-byte and anchoring to the published tag. Chose eight tags: seven over deterministic messages of block-edge sizes {0,1,15,16,17,34,256} (empty · sub-block · exact block · one over · RFC length · multi-block) with a second real implementation as oracle, plus the RFC 8439 §2.5.2 canonical message "Cryptographic Forum Research Group" anchored to the published a8061dc1305136c6c22b8baf0c0127a9
